  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 days (Through next Saturday)...\n\nLow amplitude upper ridge centered over Utah continues to dominate\nthe western CONUS. Water-vapor imagery shows monsoon moisture\nsuppressed to our south, keeping us dry while significant wildfire\nsmoke advects into our region from the Pacific NW. Generally \nspeaking, the westerly flow aloft will continue to push\nconsiderable smoke into our region from the numerous wildfires \nover the Pacific NW. \n\nThere is a weak shortwave moving across Montana overnight which\nmay produce some increase in mid/high clouds from west to east \ninto this morning. Forecast soundings show some very elevated \nmoisture and instability (near 500mb) and this should yield areas \nof high-based ACCAS and perhaps virga. Very dry boundary layer \nremains so will not mention any risk of showers. Otherwise, Sunday\nwill be not be quite as warm, dry, nor windy as yesterday. While\nwe do not have any fire weather highlights posted for our CWA, Fire\nweather conditions will remain elevated today. By tomorrow \nevening, as southeasterly low level jet develops out of western \nSD, we could see a few weak t-storms pop up in far southeast MT. \nMonday & Tuesday will remain warm (upper 80s to mid 90s), but \nmodel progs indicate PWATs will gradually increase, mainly due to\nadvection of moisture from the Great Plains. Any chance of \nconvection on these days will be low (10-20%) and confined to \nareas near the Dakota borders while the western section of our CWA\nremains much drier with RH readings in the low-mid teens. \n\nBy Wednesday, easterly low level flow will have PWAT levels \nback up to around an inch as far west as Billings, and this is \nbacked up by majority of ensemble members. Upper to mid level flow\nbacks to the southwest as an upper trough drops into the Pacific\nNW. This could allow some weak impulses to cross our area \nproviding a trigger for convection fed by the increasing moisture. \nThus, look for an increasing chance of thunderstorms by midweek\nwith high temps trending down into the 80s. \n\nExpect a greater potential for showers and t-storms along with \ncooler temps Thursday through Saturday as that aforementioned \ntrough tracks across the Canada/Montana border. Highs will be a \nlittle below normal in the 70s to lower 80s. While forcing may be \nlimited directly over our area, we should still see at least a \nchance of scattered showers/thunderstorms (20-60% coverage) with \nthe best chance on Thursday. Humidities will be seasonably high to\nend the week (mins above 40%) but the increasing risk of \nlightning could be problematic given the hot & dry weather \noccurring now. Most of our cwa has not seen measurable precip yet \nthis month, and won't over the next few days. BT",
